    "title": "Hypothetical Local Inference Speed: Beyond Raw Tokens/Second",
    "body": "This post discusses a hypothetical local inference scenario involving two configurations, achieving 18 and 24 tokens per second (t/s) respectively on the same machine with a single prompt. While these numbers offer a basic performance indicator, they represent only one facet of practical usability. Crucially, this hypothetical test did not measure time to first token (TTFT) or the quality of the generated answers. A model with higher t/s might still feel sluggish if TTFT is high, or if the output requires significant revision due to poor quality. Furthermore, the observed difference does not establish a universal winner; performance can vary greatly with different prompts, system loads, and model architectures. These synthetic figures are intended to highlight the limitations of raw t/s metrics and prompt a discussion on what other factors users consider essential for a responsive local LLM experience.",
