  {
    "title": "Interpreting Hypothetical Local LLM Speeds: What 18 vs. 24 Tokens/Second Might Mean (and Not Mean)",
    "body": "We're examining a hypothetical local inference test that yielded 18 tokens per second (t/s) on one setup and 24 t/s on another, using identical hardware and a single prompt. While a 33% increase in t/s is notable, it's vital to understand the constraints of this hypothetical data. This specific test did not capture the time until the first token was generated, nor did it assess the semantic quality or coherence of the responses. A faster generation rate doesn't automatically equate to a better user experience if the initial wait is long or the output is unsatisfactory. Moreover, this isolated test cannot be generalized; different prompts, system configurations, and model types could yield different results. The aim here is not to declare a winner but to underscore that raw throughput figures, especially from a limited hypothetical test, don't tell the whole story. What other metrics or qualitative aspects are crucial for assessing local LLM performance in your experience?",
